              397.0,
              1987.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(Alternatively, one can factor  $ r(x) $ as a product of quadratic polynomials with real coefficients, write each as a sum of squares, then multiply together to get a sum of many squares.)",
          "block_bbox": [
            318,
            2027,
            1255,
            2222
          ],
          "block_id": 10,
          "block_order": 11,
          "group_id": 10,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              318.0,
              2027.0
            ],
            [
              1255.0,
              2027.0
            ],
            [
              1255.0,
              2222.0
            ],
            [
              318.0,
              2222.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Second solution: We proceed by induction on the degree of $p$, with base case where $p$ has degree 0. As in the first solution, we may reduce to a smaller degree in case $p$ has any real roots, so assume it has none. Then $p(x) > 0$ for all real $x$, and since $p(x) \\to \\infty$ for $x \\to \\pm\\infty$, $p$ has a minimum value $c$. Now $p(x) - c$ has real roots, so as above, we deduce that $p(x) - c$ is a sum of squares. Now add one more square, namely $(\\sqrt{c})^{2}$, to get $p(x)$ as a sum of squares.",
          "block_bbox": [
            311,
            2240,
            1252,
            2676
          ],
          "block_id": 11,
          "block_order": 12,
          "group_id": 11,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              311.0,
              2240.0
            ],
            [
              1252.0,
              2240.0
            ],
            [
              1252.0,
              2676.0
            ],
            [
              311.0,
              2676.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "A–3 First solution: Computing the coefficient of  $ x^{n+1} $ in the identity  $ (1 - 2x - x^{2}) \\sum_{m=0}^{\\infty} a_{m} x^{m} = 1 $ yields the recurrence  $ a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} + a_{n-1} $; the sequence  $ \\{a_{n}\\} $ is then characterized by this recurrence and the initial conditions  $ a_{0} = 1, a_{1} = 2 $.",
          "block_bbox": [
            230,
            2714,
            1252,
            2953
          ],
          "block_id": 12,
          "block_order": 13,
          "group_id": 12,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              230.0,
              2714.0
            ],
            [
              1252.0,
              2714.0
            ],
            [
              1252.0,
              2953.0
            ],
            [
              230.0,
              2953.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Define the sequence $\\{b_n\\}$ by $b_{2n}=a_{n-1}^2+a_n^2$, $b_{2n+1}=a_n(a_{n-1}+a_{n+1})$. Then",
          "block_bbox": [
            318,
